WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.760
A hundred years ago, American chestnut trees were common in parts of Virginia's Blue

2
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.940
Ridge Mountains. Then a fungus arrived from Asia.

3
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.020
By about 1950, it had killed billions of these trees.

4
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.660
Yet small sprouts still grow from old chestnut roots.

5
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.060
Why do some species survive big changes, while others disappear?

6
00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:29.543
When an environment changes, members of a population do not share one fate.

7
00:00:30.423 --> 00:00:31.743
Some survive and reproduce.

8
00:00:32.643 --> 00:00:35.103
Some migrate, which means they move to a new place.

9
00:00:36.103 --> 00:00:41.003
Some die. A species is extinct when no living members are left anywhere.

10
00:00:41.963 --> 00:00:44.123
Vanishing from one forest is not extinction.

11
00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:48.337
Here are four changes that challenge populations today.

12
00:00:49.457 --> 00:00:53.037
Habitat loss is the destruction of the place where an organism lives.

13
00:00:54.037 --> 00:00:57.237
Pollution is harmful material added to air, water or soil.

14
00:00:58.237 --> 00:01:01.617
Climate change is a long shift in the usual weather of a region.

15
00:01:02.477 --> 00:01:06.817
An invasive species is a living thing brought to a new place, where it spreads and

16
00:01:06.817 --> 00:01:10.057
causes harm. The chestnut blight fungus is one.

17
00:01:11.017 --> 00:01:15.897
Genetic variation means inherited differences among members of a population.

18
00:01:16.997 --> 00:01:21.037
Resistance is the ability to hold off a disease better than others can.

19
00:01:21.957 --> 00:01:26.877
When a new disease arrives, a population with more variation is more likely to have

20
00:01:26.877 --> 00:01:28.457
some members that resist it.

21
00:01:29.137 --> 00:01:31.977
Those members survive and pass their traits to their offspring.

22
00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:35.250
Variation does not promise survival.

23
00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:39.950
When a change comes fast, the helpful trait usually has to be there already.

24
00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:45.470
American chestnuts had very little resistance to this fungus, so almost all the big

25
00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:50.990
trees died. The fungus can kill the trunk above the ground, but the roots, safe in

26
00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.470
the soil, often live and send up new sprouts.

27
00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.310
The new sprouts get infected and die back.

28
00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.903
A few survivors do not make a species safe.

29
00:02:00.783 --> 00:02:04.503
So why do some species survive big changes, while others disappear?

30
00:02:05.383 --> 00:02:09.883
They are more likely to survive when some members already carry a helpful trait, and

31
00:02:09.883 --> 00:02:11.023
those members reproduce.

32
00:02:11.963 --> 00:02:15.803
If no member can survive the change, the species may become extinct.
